=========================================
Day 11 - Linux Storage Management (LVM)
Lab Log
Date: YYYY-MM-DD
Host: ubuntu
=========================================

$ lsblk

NAME   MAJ:MIN RM  SIZE RO TYPE MOUNTPOINT
sda      8:0    0   50G  0 disk
├─sda1   8:1    0    1G  0 part /boot
└─sda2   8:2    0   49G  0 part /
sdb      8:16   0   20G  0 disk

----------------------------------------------------
Step 1 - Create Physical Volume
----------------------------------------------------

$ sudo pvcreate /dev/sdb

Physical volume "/dev/sdb" successfully created.

$ sudo pvs

PV         VG   Fmt  Attr PSize   PFree
/dev/sdb        lvm2 ---   20.00g 20.00g

$ sudo pvdisplay

--- Physical volume ---
PV Name               /dev/sdb
VG Name
PV Size               20.00 GiB
Allocatable           yes

----------------------------------------------------
Step 2 - Create Volume Group
----------------------------------------------------

$ sudo vgcreate dev_vg /dev/sdb

Volume group "dev_vg" successfully created

$ sudo vgs

VG      #PV #LV #SN Attr   VSize   VFree
dev_vg    1   0   0 wz--n- 20.00g 20.00g

$ sudo vgdisplay

--- Volume group ---
VG Name               dev_vg
VG Size               20.00 GiB
Free PE / Size        20.00 GiB

----------------------------------------------------
Step 3 - Create Logical Volume
----------------------------------------------------

$ sudo lvcreate -L 5G -n data_lv dev_vg

Logical volume "data_lv" created.

$ sudo lvs

LV       VG      Attr       LSize
data_lv  dev_vg  -wi-a----- 5.00g

$ sudo lvdisplay

--- Logical volume ---
LV Path                /dev/dev_vg/data_lv
LV Size                5.00 GiB

----------------------------------------------------
Step 4 - Create Filesystem
----------------------------------------------------

$ sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/dev_vg/data_lv

Creating filesystem with 1310720 4k blocks...
Writing inode tables: done
Creating journal: done
Writing superblocks and filesystem accounting information: done

----------------------------------------------------
Step 5 - Create Mount Point
----------------------------------------------------

$ sudo mkdir /data

----------------------------------------------------
Step 6 - Mount Filesystem
----------------------------------------------------

$ sudo mount /dev/dev_vg/data_lv /data

$ df -h

Filesystem                  Size Used Avail Use% Mounted on
/dev/sda2                    49G  12G   35G  26% /
/dev/mapper/dev_vg-data_lv  4.9G   24M  4.6G   1% /data

----------------------------------------------------
Verify Storage Layout
----------------------------------------------------

$ lsblk

NAME                     SIZE TYPE MOUNTPOINT
sda                       50G disk
├─sda1                     1G part /boot
└─sda2                    49G part /
sdb                       20G disk
└─dev_vg-data_lv           5G lvm  /data

----------------------------------------------------
Simulate Storage Expansion
----------------------------------------------------

New disk attached:

/dev/sdc (20G)

$ sudo pvcreate /dev/sdc

Physical volume "/dev/sdc" successfully created.

$ sudo vgextend dev_vg /dev/sdc

Volume group "dev_vg" successfully extended.

$ sudo vgs

VG      #PV #LV Attr   VSize   VFree
dev_vg    2   1 wz--n- 40.00g 35.00g

----------------------------------------------------
Extend Logical Volume
----------------------------------------------------

$ sudo lvextend -L +5G /dev/dev_vg/data_lv

Size of logical volume dev_vg/data_lv changed
Logical volume successfully resized.

----------------------------------------------------
Resize Filesystem
----------------------------------------------------

$ sudo resize2fs /dev/dev_vg/data_lv

resize2fs 1.46.5
Filesystem resized successfully.

----------------------------------------------------
Verify New Capacity
----------------------------------------------------

$ df -h

Filesystem                  Size Used Avail Use% Mounted on
/dev/mapper/dev_vg-data_lv  9.8G   24M  9.2G   1% /data

----------------------------------------------------
Useful Commands
----------------------------------------------------

$ pvs
$ pvdisplay

$ vgs
$ vgdisplay

$ lvs
$ lvdisplay

$ sudo lvmdiskscan
